  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py` and the tests pass. Running `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` ran 13 tests, all OK. Nothing is committed.\n\n- **Function:** `split(cents: int, parts: int) -> list[int]`. For example, `split(10, 3)` returns `[4, 3, 3]`. If `parts < 1` it raises `ValueError(\"parts must be at least 1: ...\")`, worded like the other errors in that file.\n- **Tests:** there was no test file for `money`, so I created `tests/test_money.py` in the same `unittest` style as `tests/test_pricing.py`. It covers:\n  - even splits\n  - the extra cents going to the front\n  - fewer cents than parts\n  - a single part\n  - the parts always summing to `cents`\n  - `ValueError` when `parts` is 0 or negative\n\n**Decision for you:** negative amounts behave in a way you might not expect. `split(-10, 3)` returns `[-3, -3, -4]`. The total is right, but the first elements are the ones closer to zero, so the extra cent ends up last. If you'd prefer `[-4, -3, -3]`, so a refund splits the same way as a charge, I can change it and add a test. Neither the function nor the tests handle negative amounts specially right now.",
